Idea: **Title**: Quantifying Intermittency-Induced Deviations from Lévy-Stable Scaling in Turbulent Transport

This study defines the effective fractional operator index $\alpha$ as a function of the multifractal intermittency parameter $\mu$, testing the robustness of the renormalization group (RG) fixed point $\alpha = 2/(1+\zeta_2)$ against non-Gaussian cascade statistics. By computing the structure functions $S_p(r)$ from the `cascade_logN_realistic` datasets, we first resolve the dependence of the spectral exponent $\xi = 1 + \zeta_2$ on $\mu$. Subsequently, we analyze the Lagrangian tracer characteristic functions $\phi(k, \tau)$ from the Kolmogorov-synthetic datasets to extract the effective diffusion exponent $\alpha_{eff}(\mu)$ as a function of the intermittency correction. We will test the hypothesis that multifractality induces a measurable departure from the ideal Lévy-stable characteristic form $\exp(-D_\alpha |k|^\alpha \tau)$, quantifying this deviation as a function of $\mu$ through the collapse of the PDF tails. This approach replaces the ungrounded distribution-of-$\alpha$ model with a rigorous sensitivity analysis of the fractional diffusion operator to the Eulerian intermittency spectrum, effectively mapping the transition from K41-idealized scaling to realistic, intermittent turbulent transport.


